<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tagus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tagus is the longest river of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, flowing westwards through central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and reaching the Atlantic at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> it formed an approximate frontier zone between Christian kingdoms and Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> use it to mark the northern limit of an empire that at its height stretched from the Atlantic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> to the Tagus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tagus.jpg" alt="Tagus" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mheberger · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tagus_Watershed.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
